{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:26.627Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"143","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Superseded by b3694159. writeSummaryLine no longer builds a line first: it wraps encoder.out in boundedSummaryWriter and streams through json.NewEncoder(bounded), so the summary is never held beside the struct it encodes. The anchored `line, err := compactJSONLine(value)` is gone from this path (compactJSONLine now serves only the dictionary line). That is this finding's own prescribed remedy - enforce the limit with a bounded/counting writer while streaming.\n\nHonest scope of the proof: the change is allocation behaviour and is byte-identical on the wire, so no test can separate it; this is a source-level argument, not a revert proof. The ceiling it enforces is covered behaviourally by TestCompactSnapshotEncoderRefusesSummaryPastTheCeiling.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1P0VTHC34QDWPXMY0AFV53D","targetId":"01M1P0VTHRD5M8797CPDAHT2J4","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:27.761Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"144","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed in 700cc67c. boundedSummaryWriter.Write now returns io.ErrShortWrite on `err == nil \u0026\u0026 written != len(payload)`, so a writer that silently accepts fewer bytes cannot leave a truncated terminal summary behind a successful Encode.\n\nProof: compile-clean revert of that one condition to `if false` -\u003e TestCompactSnapshotEncoderReportsAShortSummaryWrite fails with\n    compact_snapshot_test.go:830: err = \u003cnil\u003e, want io.ErrShortWrite","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1P0C1DQFD9XWFW5C5ESPME1","targetId":"01M1P0C1E7H02BJ1BSF2R7VS59","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:28.894Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"145","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed in 2b08c438. compactSnapshotLineIsSummary reads the record TAG structurally instead of matching the 5-byte prefix: it skips spaces and tabs around the `[`, the quoted `m` and the `,`, so `[ \"m\" , {...}]` is recognised as the same record and gets the summary allowance.\n\nProof: compile-clean revert to a `Peek(5) == [\"m\",` prefix match -\u003e TestCompactSnapshotSummaryTagIsReadStructurally fails with\n    compact_snapshot_test.go:732: \"[ \\\"m\\\" ,\" not recognised as a summary","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NZ7RJ2VJEZXDN4ZEB69VY9","targetId":"01M1NZ7RJFNG0AB06XMKW9G6EQ","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:30.121Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"146","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Superseded by 56a7685d + 4bce881f. Both halves of the premise are gone.\n\nThe 512 MiB constant no longer exists; the bound is compactSnapshotSummaryLineCeiling = 128 MiB. More importantly the summary is no longer ACCUMULATED at all: decodeCompactSummaryLine decodes it straight off the stream through encoding/json's streaming decoder behind an io.LimitReader, so nothing past the limit is ever read and the raw bytes are never held alongside the struct. That is this finding's own prescribed remedy - incremental decoding rather than a bigger or smaller buffer.\n\nProof: compile-clean revert of the io.LimitReader to a plain reader -\u003e TestCompactSnapshotSummaryStreamRefusesPastTheLimit fails with\n    compact_snapshot_test.go:703: expected an over-limit summary to be refused","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NYE5286F2WWSB6XM6DQE0R","targetId":"01M1NYE52SCGD38ZV1FGJMZ4GJ","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:31.355Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"147","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Superseded by 4bce881f. This was the central defect of the compact-snapshot cluster and both halves of it are now closed.\n\n(1) The bound is no longer derived from the READING process's file cap. compactSnapshotSummaryBytesPerFile is deleted - `grep -rn compactSnapshotSummaryBytesPerFile internal/` returns nothing - and compact_snapshot.go now references no MaxSourceFiles / MAX_FILES / MaxIndexedFiles at all. The limit is the fixed constant compactSnapshotSummaryLineCeiling.\n\n(2) The encoder DOES enforce it. writeSummaryLine refuses to emit past the ceiling, which makes it an invariant of the artifact rather than a policy of the decoder: a snapshot this build writes is a snapshot this build reads, whatever listing cap either side ran with. The write-successfully-then-unreadable scenario can no longer be constructed, and the readable-on-one-machine-not-another failure this finding names is structurally impossible.\n\nProof: compile-clean revert of the encoder-side guard -\u003e TestCompactSnapshotEncoderRefusesSummaryPastTheCeiling fails with\n    compact_snapshot_test.go:788: expected an over-ceiling summary to be refused","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NX2ZQSMNYRS39TD8BFM41E","targetId":"01M1NX2ZRX5PRWSFS3K61H6EJF","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:32.505Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"148","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Superseded - and a duplicate. This is the same defect as 01M1NYE52SCGD38ZV1FGJMZ4GJ and 01M1NS4KB17N9H651Q5DA9GJ1Z, reported against an earlier revision of this branch at a different line. The anchored `compactSnapshotSummaryLineBytes = 512 * 1024 * 1024` no longer exists.\n\nThree independent later commits retired it: 76823f89 gates the larger allowance on `seenHeader \u0026\u0026 !seenSummary`, so a five-byte prefix alone never buys it; 56a7685d decodes the summary off the stream instead of accumulating a line, so the allowance bounds a streaming read rather than a buffer; 4bce881f makes the now-128 MiB ceiling a two-sided invariant enforced by the encoder. Revert proofs are recorded on those two findings.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NTX6XYZ224H9BA0Q7A8016","targetId":"01M1NTX6YFDTWRFMP2PZT46GKA","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:33.668Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"149","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed in 76823f89, exactly as prescribed. The expanded allowance is reachable only `if seenHeader \u0026\u0026 !seenSummary` - after a validated header, at a position where a summary is legal, once per decode. Every other line goes through readCompactSnapshotLine with the flat 16 MiB compactSnapshotRecordLineBytes, so a malformed artifact that merely OPENS with the summary prefix gets 16 MiB, not the ceiling.\n\nProof: compile-clean revert to `if !seenSummary` (dropping the header requirement) -\u003e TestCompactSnapshotSummaryAllowanceRequiresAHeader fails with\n    compact_snapshot_test.go:722: decode error = \"compact snapshot summary: invalid character 'x' looking for beginning of value\", want category containing \"compact snapshot line exceeds\"","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NS4KAFYD54A7D47K83NXJM","targetId":"01M1NS4KB17N9H651Q5DA9GJ1Z","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:34.767Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"150","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Superseded by f731ab38 + 76823f89 + 56a7685d. The anchored `limit = -1` is gone; there is no unlimited path left in the decoder.\n\nVerified rather than assumed: production calls readCompactSnapshotLine exactly once, at compact_snapshot.go:1146, with compactSnapshotRecordLineBytes (16 MiB) - every other call site is in tests. The larger summary allowance is finite (128 MiB), gated on a validated header at a legal summary position, and applies to a streaming decode rather than an append loop. The prescribed remedy, \"retain a bounded/streaming summary parser\", is precisely what 56a7685d implemented in decodeCompactSummaryLine.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NQR79EHGT7W4J8WJRKFZNB","targetId":"01M1NQR79M6F7HYD5CAJSVPNZC","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:36.222Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"151","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed in f731ab38, exactly as prescribed (\"keep a small per-record limit for header/data lines and apply the derived allowance only to the summary\"). The anchored `readCompactSnapshotLine(reader, compactSnapshotLineLimit(fileRecords))` is now `readCompactSnapshotLine(reader, compactSnapshotRecordLineBytes)` - a flat 16 MiB on every non-summary line - so an unterminated first line costs 16 MiB rather than the ~391 MiB this finding measured.\n\nProof: compile-clean revert to an unbounded limit -\u003e TestCompactSnapshotDecoderRejectsOversizedNonSummaryLine fails with\n    compact_snapshot_test.go:510: decode error = \"compact snapshot line 1: invalid character 'x' looking for beginning of value\", want category containing \"compact snapshot line exceeds\"","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NPV1S12VQAGDBX5PXK4G3K","targetId":"01M1NPV1S6XMBT3CW3S89VH0RR","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:37.387Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"152","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Superseded by 4bce881f. The anchored `const compactSnapshotSummaryBytesPerFile = 2048` is deleted, so the premise no longer applies: the bound is not per-file any more and no longer depends on a path-length assumption that the encoder never guaranteed.\n\nThe finding's alternative remedy is the one taken - \"enforce the same limit during encoding\". writeSummaryLine now refuses to emit past compactSnapshotSummaryLineCeiling, so encoder and decoder agree by construction rather than by arithmetic over a configured file count, and a small ENTIRE_GRAPH_MAX_FILES can no longer make a valid snapshot unreadable.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NPK48017X5GB5NE1S82QD7","targetId":"01M1NPK486ZAQCWXRQ5FPZ4KHP","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:38.604Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"153","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Superseded by 4bce881f - the multiplication is gone, so the overflow is unreachable rather than guarded.\n\nThe anchored `return compactSnapshotSummaryBytesPerFile * files` and the constant it multiplied no longer exist (`grep -rn compactSnapshotSummaryBytesPerFile internal/` -\u003e no hits). The summary bound is now the fixed constant compactSnapshotSummaryLineCeiling and is computed from nothing, so ENTIRE_GRAPH_MAX_FILES cannot influence it at any magnitude and there is no product left to overflow to zero or negative.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NNMYYHJ4ZRWGMYEBCW81YH","targetId":"01M1NNMYZ0RMAPG8YKAXCEHMQP","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:39.718Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"154","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Superseded by 4bce881f. Recording the reasoning, because this finding is correct on the tension and one of its two prescribed remedies was deliberately REJECTED.\n\nThe finding is right that a fixed decoder bound cannot on its own read every snapshot an unbounded encoder can write. Remedy 1, deriving the decoder bound from configuration, was rejected: the process that reads a snapshot need not share the listing cap of the process that wrote it, so the same artifact would be readable on one machine and not another - which is the separate defect reported as 01M1NX2ZRX5PRWSFS3K61H6EJF. Remedy 2, streaming summaries across bounded records, is a format change.\n\nThe fix closes the gap from the other end instead: writeSummaryLine refuses to emit past compactSnapshotSummaryLineCeiling, so the encoder cannot produce a snapshot this decoder rejects, and the ceiling becomes an invariant of the FORMAT rather than a policy of either side. An over-ceiling corpus now fails loudly at encode time - an error a caller can act on - instead of writing a file that cannot later be read. Measured headroom recorded in-code: 90,000 partial failures encode to 24.4 MB (~270 bytes/entry), so 128 MiB holds roughly two million, an order of magnitude past what a repository at the default listing cap can produce even if every file fails.\n\nProof: compile-clean revert of the encoder guard -\u003e TestCompactSnapshotEncoderRefusesSummaryPastTheCeiling fails with\n    compact_snapshot_test.go:788: expected an over-ceiling summary to be refused","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NMVGZ60RF8EPA2E4C15V1T","targetId":"01M1NMVGZB9KMQ1XMXQXEK9C7E","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:40.931Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"155","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed in f731ab38, exactly as prescribed. readCompactSnapshotLine checks the bound BEFORE the append, so an over-long line is refused rather than accumulated:\n\n    if limit \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 len(line)+len(chunk) \u003e limit {\n        return nil, fmt.Errorf(\"compact snapshot line exceeds %d bytes\", limit)\n    }\n    line = append(line, chunk...)\n\nThe decode path passes compactSnapshotRecordLineBytes (16 MiB), and that is large enough for every record the encoder emits - the summary, the one line that grows with the corpus, no longer travels this path at all (56a7685d streams it).\n\nProof: compile-clean revert to an unbounded limit -\u003e TestCompactSnapshotDecoderRejectsOversizedNonSummaryLine fails with\n    compact_snapshot_test.go:510: ... want category containing \"compact snapshot line exceeds\"","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NKSZBEJ0FR8GK7QVPP213N","targetId":"01M1NKSZBN8Z7MFZXQK5GHS4MV","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:42.039Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"156","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed in e80904e6, exactly as prescribed (\"skip a TypeScript return annotation before checking for `{`\"). javascriptDefaultExportBodyBrace now detects a `:` after the parameter list and hands off to typeScriptAnnotatedBodyBrace instead of requiring the body brace to follow the closing paren.\n\nProof: compile-clean revert removing that branch -\u003e TestJavaScriptDefaultExportExpressionArrowKeepsItsOwnRange fails with\n    parser_default_export_regression_test.go:99: annotated end line = 1, want 3 for \"export default function (): Result {\\n  return build()\\n}\\n\"\nwhich is the exact one-line range this finding predicted.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NQR79EHGT7W4J8WJRKFZNB","targetId":"01M1NQR79RVBG28V0DVMAYJ8HT","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:43.219Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"157","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed in e80904e6, exactly as prescribed (\"locate the brace after the complete return type\"). typeScriptAnnotatedBodyBrace skips any brace that typeScriptTypeContinues reports as still inside the type, stepping over it with matchingDelimiterOffset, so `(): { ok: boolean } { ... }` resolves to the body brace and not the type's.\n\nProof: compile-clean revert making it return the first `{` after the `:` -\u003e two failures:\n    parser_default_export_regression_test.go:99: annotated end line = 1, want 3 for \"export default function (): { ok: boolean } {\\n  return { ok: true }\\n}\\n\"\n    parser_default_export_regression_test.go:209: typeScriptAnnotatedBodyBrace(\": { ok: boolean } { body }\") = 2, want 18","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NVG0RY7TG0SNQRX57QABY9","targetId":"01M1NVG0SF6DT7D1CZM0T9ZRJT","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:44.350Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"158","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed in 1f9cd73e, and this finding's own snippet is now a regression case. typeScriptTypeContinues treats `[` (tuple type) and `?` (the true branch of a conditional type) as leaving the type expression unfinished, so the brace after them is still part of the type:\n\n    case ':', '|', '\u0026', '\u003c', ',', '(', '[', '?':\n\nProof: compile-clean revert dropping `'['` and `'?'` from that case -\u003e two failures:\n    parser_default_export_regression_test.go:99: annotated end line = 1, want 3 for \"export default function (): [{ ok: boolean }] {\\n  return build()\\n}\\n\"\n    parser_default_export_regression_test.go:209: typeScriptAnnotatedBodyBrace(\": [{ ok: boolean }] { body }\") = 3, want 20\nThe first is the finding's example verbatim. The conditional-type case `: T extends U ? { ok: boolean } : { ok: false }` is covered in the same tables.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NWFAAQQBSNABZFE30XGH48","targetId":"01M1NWFAB3F29XZ686FNAK9DY3","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:45.544Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"159","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed in ec5b4821, exactly as prescribed (\"the bridge must stay within the remaining budget\"). The anchored call now passes only what preselection left unspent:\n\n    selection.files = bridgeRegistrationHandlerFiles(ctx, source, selection.files, options.MaxIndexedFiles-len(selection.files))\n\nand bridgeRegistrationHandlerFiles returns the selection untouched on `budget \u003c= 0`.\n\nProof: compile-clean revert passing searchRegistrationBridgeMaxHandlers instead of the remainder -\u003e TestSearchBridgeRespectsExplicitIndexLimit fails.\n\nChecked for a second instance and it is NOT one: the other call site (search.go:1917, the git-tree-grep branch) passes its own budget because that path deliberately does not apply MaxIndexedFiles at all - it keeps every matched file, MaxIndexedFiles being a cold selective-indexing guard rather than a retrieval recall cap once the graph is preindexed. The documented exact-compatibility limit this finding is about is the preselection path, and that path now honours it.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NNMYYHJ4ZRWGMYEBCW81YH","targetId":"01M1NNMYYR9P8RQDAAC9H81AC2","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:46.671Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"160","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed in 4bce881f, exactly as prescribed (\"mark selected definitions as placed before applying the budget\"). Handlers the selection already defines are removed FIRST, and only then is the budget applied:\n\n    handlers = unplacedRegistrationHandlers(handlers, placedRegistrationHandlers(handlers, selected, source.read))\n    if len(handlers) \u003e budget {\n        handlers = handlers[:budget]\n    }\n\nso the budget-1 case in this finding - an already-covered, lexicographically earlier handler eating the only slot - can no longer occur.\n\nProof: compile-clean revert swapping the two statements back to truncate-then-dedupe -\u003e TestBridgeRegistrationHandlerFilesSkipsAlreadyCoveredHandlers fails.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NX2ZQSMNYRS39TD8BFM41E","targetId":"01M1NX2ZRDPVZMDY13MCFRRKWB","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:47.767Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"161","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed in f731ab38, as prescribed (\"additions must distinguish definitions\"). A file is added only when topLevelFunctionNames reports that it DEFINES the handler, so a call site can no longer consume a bridge slot; b4407620 additionally stops call sites reaching the parse at all, so they do not consume the scan budget either.\n\nProof: compile-clean revert dropping the `!defined[handler]` gate -\u003e TestBridgeRegistrationHandlerFilesHonoursAPartialBudget fails with\n    search_registration_bridge_test.go:290: bridged with budget 2 = [src/commands/append.json src/commands/substr.json src/t_append.go], want [src/commands/append.json src/commands/substr.json src/t_append.go src/t_string.go]\n- a handler left unbridged because a non-defining file took its slot, which is exactly the recall failure this finding describes.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NKSZBEJ0FR8GK7QVPP213N","targetId":"01M1NKSZBVZRGMQEX1PYD6BYXD","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:48.925Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"162","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed in b4407620, exactly as prescribed (\"call sites should not exhaust the definition-search budget\"). The cheap screen in front of the parse is now declaresAppliedIdentifier, which requires the occurrence to read as a DECLARATION: everything before the name on its line must be a declaration's leading tokens, so any operator, paren, dot or bracket rules it out, as does a statement keyword or a line ending in a semicolon. Call sites are therefore rejected before `parsed++`, and cannot spend the 64-file parse budget.\n\nProof: compile-clean revert restoring the call-permissive screen -\u003e TestBridgeRegistrationHandlerFilesOutlastsManyCallers fails. That test places 12 lexically earlier calling files ahead of the defining file, which is the shape this finding describes.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NT43Q6NSYXG2F6NRP8A5HY","targetId":"01M1NT43QKX2WJ2QMPW95415J1","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:33:50.108Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"163","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed in f731ab38, as prescribed (\"verify an appropriate top-level definition\"). Matching is no longer by unqualified name: topLevelFunctionNames returns only TOP-LEVEL function definitions, so a method or a nested function that merely shares the handler's name neither marks it placed nor holds its slot.\n\nTestTopLevelFunctionNames asserts both exclusions directly - `func (s Store) getrangeCommand() {}` must not be reported, and a `getrangeCommand` nested inside `outer()` must not be reported. TestBridgeRegistrationHandlerFilesSkipsSameNamedMethod puts the same-named method lexically ahead of the real definition (src/aaa_store.go before src/t_string.go) and requires src/t_string.go to still be bridged.\n\nProof that the gate is load-bearing rather than redundant: compile-clean revert dropping `!defined[handler]` -\u003e TestBridgeRegistrationHandlerFilesHonoursAPartialBudget fails at search_registration_bridge_test.go:290 with a handler left unbridged.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NPK48017X5GB5NE1S82QD7","targetId":"01M1NPK48AVC7H9WWK4HR0N787","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NKR7N8YJBCBHH4FC2WEJZ3"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
